## 5. Layer-number overrides (advanced, optional)

You were right to be cautious here. Baking layer numbers into flag *names* does not scale well.

The better model is **override directives**.

### Proposal: repeatable override flags

```
--dep-layer-no-comments <N>
--dep-layer-no-fn-bodies <N>
--dep-layer-no-tests <N>
--dep-layer-no-test-bodies <N>
```

Properties:

* repeatable
* explicit
* sparse (you only use them when you need them)
* do not clutter the common path

Example:

```
--deps-no-fn-bodies
--dep-layer-no-fn-bodies 0
```

Or:

```
--deps-no-comments
--dep-layer-no-comments 2
```

These act as **overrides on top of role defaults**.

Precedence (important, predictable):

```
layer override > role override > global dep default > main default
```

---

## 6. Subtree pruning (this is cleanly orthogonal)

Two distinct but related needs:

### A. Omit specific crates entirely

```
--omit-dep <crate-name>
```

Repeatable.

Semantics:

* Do not emit this crate
* Do not emit its dependency subtree
* Still allow other paths to resolve types by name

### B. Omit entire subtrees explicitly

This is semantically identical, but clearer for intent:

```
--omit-dep-subtree <crate-name>
```

In practice, you might implement both as the same thing internally, but having both names is *good UX*.

---

## 7. Layer pruning (coarse but powerful)

This is complementary to subtree pruning:

```
--max-dep-layer <N>
--min-dep-layer <N>
--only-dep-layer <N>
```

Semantics:

* Applied *after* dependency resolution
* Acts like an implicit subtree prune
* Plays very well with your layered architecture

Example:

```
